  "about": "Metro's typical timetable (core.transit.typical_metro): what Metrorail plans use while no WMATA rail GTFS is built into the installed schedule; a schedule with WMATA's own GTFS replaces it. The owner, 2026-10-12: \"Just use defaults. The wmata website has a typical timetable for rail.\" `hours`: open and close per day type, service-day clock (26:00 is 2 am the next morning); `holiday`: the day type a federal holiday runs (an assumption: WMATA publishes each holiday's hours separately). `lines`: minutes between trains per time band, as each line's wmata.com info page gives them (read 2026-10-12; [a, b] is \"every a-b min\"; open and close are the day's hours). `services`: each line's stops in order; a line with two services alternates its trains between them (Silver: half to New Carrollton; Yellow: half to Greenbelt; frontend/src/rail-data/metro-line-corrections.json cites WMATA for both). `run_s`: seconds from each station to the next, an estimate (wmata.com gives no run times): the straight line between the two stations at 33 mph (53 km/h), Metro's average speed including stops, at least 90 s, to the nearest 10 s (docs/SOURCES.md). `stations`: the map's 98 Metro stations with each one's bike entrance and its count of street elevators known (`street_elevators`), as scripts/build_transit_schedule.py metro_stations() reads them from frontend/src/rail-data/ (tests/test_transit.py holds the two equal).",
